---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import PageHero from "../../components/PageHero.astro";
import CompareTable from "../../components/CompareTable.astro";
import CtaBand from "../../components/CtaBand.astro";

const rows = [
  { label: "Actively looks for copies of your content", them: true, us: "On demand, by paste or URL" },
  { label: "Finds lightly reworded lookalikes", them: true, us: true },
  { label: "Shows that two pages match", them: true, us: true },
  { label: "Shows whose text it was first", them: false, us: true },
  { label: "Mark embedded in the content itself", them: false, us: true },
  { label: "Works on a copy pasted outside the scanned sites", them: false, us: true },
  { label: "Produces an ownership document for a takedown", them: "Reports of matches", us: true },
];
---

<Base
  title="Kirpik vs copy monitors and plagiarism scanners"
  description="Copy monitors find pages that match yours. A match is not ownership: it shows similarity, not origin. Why monitoring and text watermarking answer different questions, and how they combine."
>
  <PageHero
    title="Copy monitors vs proof of ownership"
    lead="Monitoring services scan other sites for text that matches yours and raise a flag when they find it. Genuinely useful, and closer to the real problem than any blocker. But a match answers the question that text matches. It leaves open the one that wins disputes: whose text is it?"
  />

  <section class="section" style="padding-top:0">
    <div class="container narrow prose">
      <h2>What monitoring does well</h2>
      <p>
        A monitor works server-side, comparing content from sites it scans against yours, the same
        family of techniques as plagiarism scanners. Good ones catch partial matches and light
        rewording, and they do the tedious part for you: watching. In the Shopify anti-theft category we
        surveyed in June and July 2026, this approach is rare and worth naming. Spy Blocker (the copycat
        monitor by FlowIntegrity Drive) scans domains you choose for "copied images, copied text, and
        similar product pages" and assembles evidence packs. IP Moat runs marketplace scanning with a
        managed takedown service on top. Mintall Keep lists IP monitoring alongside its provenance
        records.
      </p>
      <p>
        Notably, these monitors are among the only apps in the category that engage with text at all.
        The seventy-odd blocker apps never leave the browser tab; a monitor at least goes looking where
        stolen copy actually ends up.
      </p>

      <h2>What a match is, and is not</h2>
      <p>
        A match is a similarity report: page A and page B carry substantially the same words. It says
        nothing, on its own, about direction. Did they copy you, did you copy them, did you both source
        the same manufacturer text? You know your own answer, but knowing is not showing. A host,
        marketplace or search engine processing a takedown wants evidence of ownership and priority, and
        a similarity score does not carry either. The monitor has found the fire; you still have to prove
        whose house it is.
      </p>
      <p>
        There is also a coverage ceiling. Monitoring sees the sites it scans. A copy pasted into a
        marketplace listing, a social shop or a site outside the scan set stays invisible until someone
        stumbles on it.
      </p>

      <h2>The missing half: a mark inside the text</h2>
      <p>
        Kirpik approaches the same problem from inside the content. Its invisible algorithmic watermark,
        derived from your original text and tied to your store's unique watermark code, travels inside
        every copy of your words. When you check a suspect page or paste a sample, detection does not
        report "similar". It reports "this text carries this store's watermark", with a count of
        surviving security markers and a confidence figure. The Chain of Proof certificate then lays out
        fingerprints, dates and an Internet Archive timeline in a document anyone can verify through its
        public verification page.
      </p>
      <p>
        Monitoring tells you that text matches. A watermark proves whose text it was. If you run a
        monitor, its alerts become Kirpik's work queue: every flagged URL is one detection away from a
        certificate. The two approaches are complementary, and only one of them can be added after the
        theft. Watermark first, watch second.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" style="padding-top:0">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <CompareTable themLabel="Copy monitor" rows={rows} />
      <p class="tnote">
        The monitor column reflects scanning services as described in their own Shopify App Store
        listings, June and July 2026, including the apps named above; capabilities vary by service. App
        names and trademarks belong to their owners. Not legal advice. App-by-app pages:
        <a href="/compare/copycat-alert-product-monitor/">Spy Blocker (Copycat Monitor)</a>,
        <a href="/compare/ip-moat/">IP Moat</a>,
        <a href="/compare/mintallkeep/">Mintall Keep</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CtaBand
    heading="A match starts the argument. Proof ends it."
    sub="Kirpik reads your store's own watermark out of a stolen copy and hands you the certificate. Free to start."
  />
</Base>

<style>
  .tnote { margin-top: 1rem; font-size: 0.84rem; color: var(--ink-faint); line-height: 1.65; }
  .tnote a { color: var(--orange-deep); font-weight: 600; }
</style>
